-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Submission Contract
+
+The submission envelope contains `visitId`, `baseRevisionId`, complete `payload`, and `submissionKey`. Omitting `baseRevisionId` or setting it to `null` denotes an initial submission; it is not a way to bypass conflict detection.
+
+The server authenticates the worker, validates the envelope and scientific fields, and resolves the catalog plot UUID before creating a revision. A non-null base must belong to the same visit. A missing or unrelated base is invalid rather than being treated as an edit against current state.
+
+A successful response returns the stored `revisionId`, its `baseRevisionId`, `receivedAt`, and whether the revision became current or entered the review set. This acceptance result describes that submission's transaction, not a promise that the revision remains current after later activity.
+
+Retries use the same complete envelope and `submissionKey`. A byte-for-byte equivalent canonical envelope returns the original acceptance result. Reuse of the key with any different envelope is rejected as an idempotency conflict. Key comparison is global so accidental reuse across visits cannot return another visit's result.
+
+## Retrieval Contract
+
+Current-state retrieval returns:
+
+- the visit ID and current complete revision;
+- an `unresolved` indicator;
+- when unresolved, every revision in the outstanding review set, including complete payloads, base IDs, authors, and receipt times.
+
+Submission-history retrieval returns all revisions in receipt order, including superseded and non-winning revisions. It also returns resolution records so a reader can explain why a revision became current. Retrieving a visit never resolves or otherwise mutates its review state.
+
+## Resolution Contract
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit by supplying the visit ID, the selected revision ID from the current outstanding review set, and the complete set of revision IDs the coordinator compared. The selected complete payload becomes current without modification. No new scientific revision is created, and non-winning submissions remain in history.
+
+The expected-set comparison makes review decisions optimistic-concurrency operations. If another revision joins the set before resolution commits, the set no longer matches and the resolution is rejected as stale. The coordinator must retrieve and compare the expanded set rather than unknowingly closing an unseen submission. A revision outside the outstanding set cannot be selected.
+
+A successful resolution records the coordinator ID, server resolution time, selected revision ID, and every revision ID in the resolved set. Resolution and read-set verification are atomic with respect to new submissions for that visit. The operation clears only that outstanding set; later edits use the selected revision as current and follow the ordinary acceptance rules.
+
+The resolution itself is immutable. Correcting an earlier choice requires a new visit submission based on current state, followed by review if another worker has advanced the visit independently.
+
+## API Shape
+
+| Operation | Inputs | Success result | Main rejection cases |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Submit revision | Visit ID, optional base revision ID, complete payload, submission key | Stored revision and current/review placement | Invalid payload or plot, unknown or unrelated base, key conflict |
+| Retrieve current visit | Visit ID | Current revision, unresolved indicator, outstanding review set | Unknown visit |
+| Retrieve visit history | Visit ID | All revisions and resolutions in receipt order | Unknown visit |
+| Resolve review set | Visit ID, selected revision ID, expected complete review set | Resolution record and selected current revision | Stale set, revision outside set, visit not unresolved |
+
+Authenticated workers can submit and retrieve visits. Only coordinators can resolve review sets. Client-visible errors distinguish malformed or scientifically invalid submissions, unknown resources, idempotency-key conflicts, and stale review sets without exposing another user's submission payload in an error.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit semantics | Immutable whole-visit revisions with coordinator selection | Field-aware three-way merge; last-writer-wins | Preserves each worker's scientific intent and avoids maintained merge rules combining related edits incorrectly, at the cost of a larger review queue. |
+| Representation of a resolution | Point current state at an existing complete revision and store a separate resolution record | Create a synthetic merged revision; rewrite the selected submission | Keeps submitted observations immutable and makes the coordinator's choice auditable without implying field-level composition. |
+| Submission while review is pending | Accept into the outstanding review set | Reject further edits; automatically close or replace earlier review work | Offline workers cannot coordinate before submitting, and every valid submitted edit must remain available for review. |
+| Concurrent transaction safety | Serialize submission placement and resolution per visit; verify the exact review set at resolution | Rely on arrival order; resolve against only a selected revision | Prevents unseen concurrent submissions from being displaced by either automatic advancement or coordinator resolution. |
+| Idempotency scope | Globally unique submission keys compared against the complete envelope | Per-visit keys; payload-only comparison | Prevents cross-visit key collisions and makes base revision and authorship metadata part of retry identity. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Whole-visit revisions are the concurrency policy; field-aware three-way merge is not used.
+2. Unresolved visits retain a readable current revision but cannot enter reviewed export.
+3. Resolutions select one complete submitted revision and do not synthesize field-level combinations.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Physical storage technology, indexes, retention limits, and operational backup procedures.
+2. Pagination and filtering for large submission histories and review queues.
+3. Tablet-sync presentation of review-set growth and review-export comparison layout.
